WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:15.699
Dan Fletcher - "Being a White House Fellow"

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.732
Hi, my name is Dan Fletcher, and I'm a professor of bioengineering and biophysics at UC Berkeley,

00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:21.532
and my lab works on the cytoskeleton.

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:26.432
We're interested in how forces sculpt cellular organization and behavior.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:29.732
But, a little over a year ago, I wasn't doing that.

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:33.399
I was taking some time to work at the White House.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.399
Very different experience from working in the lab.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.099
I went to Washington, DC out of an interest in understanding

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.099
why the taxpayers are paying us to do research,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.199
how public policy makers are using the information that we are generating,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.632
and what is there that we can do to improve that process.

00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:55.932
It turned out to be a tremendous experience.

00:00:55.933 --> 00:01:02.666
I thoroughly enjoyed the time there, but it's great to be back and in a research lab.

00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:06.632
Part of the reason that I enjoyed it is that it exposed me to very different people

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:11.032
and very different experiences than I have during my normal career.

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:16.466
Most of us haven't really been outside of academia, if you've been in academia.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.166
As a result, you don't really get a chance to understand

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:23.699
some of what motivates different parts of the country,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.032
and so this was a chance to get a little bit of a perspective on that broad set of people.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:34.266
So I wanted to talk about a few of the experiences that I had in DC,

00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:42.032
but also try and identify a few thoughts on how to be effective in communicating research ideas,

00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:45.699
which is a central role for scientists in Washington, DC.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.866
So, Washington, DC is a whole new world.  Very different from Berkeley, where I live

00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:55.066
and very different from most academic environments.

00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:59.132
It's a very formal place.  I had to wear a suit and tie every day.

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:06.466
I had to go to meetings on time.  It was a very unique experience.

00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:14.432
I was even told to get a haircut, and that was by Karl Rove, and I didn't get a haircut.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:19.566
But it taught you lots of things about how people organize

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:25.332
and how people decide what policies are worthwhile.

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:27.832
There's not an experiment you can do to decide that policy.

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:30.232
It comes from conversations, from interactions.

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:33.832
And that's why cocktail parties in Washington, DC are so important.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:37.532
They're like our conferences.  There's no poster exhibit,

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:40.099
but there is a chance to exchange information.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.599
So, what does a scientist do in Washington, DC?

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:50.166
Well, my goal in part was to be a link to active research labs

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:54.166
that are producing this information that taxpayers are paying for.

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:58.532
And to provide some insight on how that process works

00:02:58.533 --> 00:03:04.566
and hopefully some specific knowledge that would be useful in policy decisions.

00:03:04.567 --> 00:03:08.999
I came to Washington, DC as part of a program called the White House Fellows Program,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.899
and it's a program that's been around since about 1965,

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.399
and it brings early career professionals from a wide variety

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.599
of disciplines to Washington, DC for an experience there.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:29.966
My class of fellows was a group of 14 people and a fantastic and varied group of people,

00:03:29.967 --> 00:03:34.466
including doctors, lawyers, businesspeople, and people from the military.

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:38.499
A fabulous experience and a wonderful group of people.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.432
So, about half of my time in Washington, DC

00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:43.799
was spent working with this group of people,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.099
and we met with over a hundred different speakers over the course of the time there,

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.232
including the President, the Vice President,

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:58.466
various cabinet secretaries, and other influential people,

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:02.899
such as columnists and activists.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.232
In those meetings, we got a chance to discuss a wide variety of issues,

00:04:07.233 --> 00:04:10.499
and it was a very educational process.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.666
The other half of my time was spent at the

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:14.099
White House Office of Science and Technology Policy,

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.199
working together with the science advisor

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.599
as well as a variety of other people in that office.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:27.399
We worked on issues that ranged from life sciences to biosecurity to health technology.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.966
Each day, more issues would be coming in,

00:04:29.967 --> 00:04:33.666
or you would be trying to push other topics forward,

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:37.632
and many of the decisions that needed to be made were, "What do we do next?"

00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:41.699
What is the right role for the government in a particular topic?

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.232
whether it's stem cell research or the requirement

00:04:45.233 --> 00:04:47.966
for certain health information to be made available.

00:04:47.967 --> 00:04:50.966
One thing I observed right away was that

00:04:50.967 --> 00:04:55.866
Washington, DC is a very busy place‚Äîa very fast paced environment.

00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:57.666
There's not a lot of time for contemplation.

00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:00.432
Things happen very quickly and people work very hard,

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.666
so I have a great deal of respect for the

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:05.499
civil servants that form the core of the government.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:11.932
In this process that is so fast paced, what is the role for science?

00:05:11.933 --> 00:05:18.632
It was really exciting to see that everywhere I went

00:05:18.633 --> 00:05:22.566
and among the people I talked with, science is something that is broadly supported.

00:05:22.567 --> 00:05:26.666
Democrats, Republicans, the White House, the Congress.

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:32.432
Science is seen as a real key to addressing national problems and national priorities.

00:05:32.433 --> 00:05:34.499
And so that was wonderful to see.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.932
What was less clear was how the information

00:05:36.933 --> 00:05:40.632
that we're producing is getting into these environments.

00:05:40.633 --> 00:05:43.699
It's to some extent through advisory councils,

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.366
to some extent through news stories,

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:48.899
but there weren't direct routes to figure out

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.866
what does the future look like, and that was surprising to me.

00:05:53.867 --> 00:05:58.066
It was also surprising and maybe a little disheartening

00:05:58.067 --> 00:05:59.999
to realize that science is not the answer.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.666
That is, science doesn't address a policy issue completely.

00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:07.532
It provides a lot of the background information,

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.132
but at some point, someone has to be a decision maker.

00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:14.699
Someone has to say, "This is how we're going to do it... Not this way."

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.032
And many times, that's a value judgment.  That's not a scientific judgment.

00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:23.099
And as a result, science is playing a role up to a point,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:27.366
and after that it's a balancing of various interests,

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:32.699
and balancing those interests is a great challenge of any system of government.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.866
Getting a perspective on where science plays a role

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:39.432
was a very rewarding part of this experience.

00:06:39.433 --> 00:06:44.899
So, this brings me to the main point, and that is,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:49.032
How can we as scientists be effective in Washington, DC?

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.199
What is our role?

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.966
I think there are five things that we can do.

00:06:52.967 --> 00:06:56.766
One is that we need to play a role in synthesizing ideas.

00:06:56.767 --> 00:07:01.499
We are all experts in specific areas, but those specific areas

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:06.666
and that particular topic is only going to part of a solution.

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:09.299
So if we can play a role in synthesizing

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.066
what we do together with what other people do,

00:07:12.067 --> 00:07:14.799
to come up with solutions, that's going to be

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.499
much more effective than just describing the work that we're doing individually.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.432
So, being able to synthesize‚Äîbeing a little broad‚Äî

00:07:22.433 --> 00:07:25.699
is very important in being effective in Washington, DC.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.999
Second is recognizing the diversity of interests.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.532
People come to Washington, DC with lots of different pulls on them.

00:07:34.533 --> 00:07:37.466
And understanding who's in favor of an idea,

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:42.332
who's against an idea is a critical part of moving a topic forward.

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:47.199
Third, something I didn't realize until a few months into this program,

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.732
was that famous people are just people.

00:07:49.733 --> 00:07:55.932
Even if you're sitting in the Oval Office or having lunch with Colin Powell, these are people.

00:07:55.933 --> 00:07:59.199
They've done amazing things, but they benefit

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:04.932
from the same sort of hard questions, the same sort of critical assessment that we do

00:08:04.933 --> 00:08:07.832
in improving ourselves and our work.

00:08:07.833 --> 00:08:11.666
And so, I think that's an important thing to remember

00:08:11.667 --> 00:08:13.299
when you're interacting with government officials

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:17.132
is they adhere to the same rules of logic that we do.

00:08:17.133 --> 00:08:23.632
Fourth is come with solutions, not just with problems.

00:08:23.633 --> 00:08:26.799
The most effective way to make progress in Washington, DC is to have a solution.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:30.532
And if you simply point out a problem with the way

00:08:30.533 --> 00:08:33.966
funding is done, or the lack of funding in a particular area,

00:08:33.967 --> 00:08:37.866
that's not a solution.  There are lots of problems the government deals with.

00:08:37.867 --> 00:08:39.899
It's much more exciting and much more productive

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.332
to come with a solution‚Äîhow to move forward on that topic.

00:08:43.333 --> 00:08:48.099
And lastly, maybe most importantly, is that we

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:52.366
as scientists have a perspective on where our fields are going,

00:08:52.367 --> 00:08:58.366
and that's the vision we need to share with the policymakers in Washington, DC.

00:08:58.367 --> 00:09:01.632
We have ideas, we have a perspective that

00:09:01.633 --> 00:09:04.966
they aren't going to have the time or the ability to have,

00:09:04.967 --> 00:09:08.166
and we need to come up with ways of sharing that perspective.

00:09:08.167 --> 00:09:12.799
A quote I like that relates to this is one piece of advice

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.866
given to Wayne Gretzky, the ice hockey player

00:09:15.867 --> 00:09:22.266
when he was told to not skate to where the puck is, but to where the puck is going to be.

00:09:22.267 --> 00:09:24.899
I think that's the big challenge in government‚Äî

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:30.932
devising policies that are going to be appropriate

00:09:30.933 --> 00:09:35.899
for where the world's going to be in 10 or 20 years.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:40.132
And we're the ones that have an idea where science is going to be in that amount of time.

00:09:40.133 --> 00:09:42.732
So, we need to play a role in conveying that idea.

00:09:42.733 --> 00:09:45.932
It was a wonderful experience to be in DC,

00:09:45.933 --> 00:09:50.332
but I'm wonderfully happy to be back in Berkeley, back in lab,

00:09:50.333 --> 00:09:57.732
and enjoying the West Coast.

00:09:57.733 --> 00:09:59.466
See more at ibiomagazine.org.

00:09:59.467 --> 01:00:00.000
